  "amendments": [
    {
      "amended_utc": "2026-08-21T17:24:26.501329+00:00",
      "author": "claude",
      "summary": "Record the per-block caveats found in analysis; measurement quality itself is the best in the dataset",
      "detail": "Block A returned eight reads of exactly 0.0000 g and the maximum per-trial sigma over all 64 trials was 0.2 mg, with zero shocks and zero retries, so nothing here is limited by the room. Three caveats are about column state and fixed block order rather than noise. (1) Block C at tilt 0 and 45 deg was still charging: the six revolutions run 9.9, 9.8, 23.8, 30.1, 32.9, 33.2 mg at 0 deg and 143 to 225 mg at 45 deg, both rising, so the block C means understate the steady state. Block E's later re-feed rotations at the same tilt and RPM give 37.4 mg/rev at 0 deg (RSD 6.2 pct) and 190.8 mg/rev at 45 deg (RSD 6.1 pct) and are the better estimates; the block C / block E ratio at 45 deg is 1.03. (2) Block C at tilt 90 deg decays instead: 319, 275, 161, 163, 138, 147 mg, so its 200.4 mg mean describes no revolution that happened and it is confounded with run order and column depletion. (3) Block D's per-speed split is not usable for this powder: 261.0, 45.1, 136.2 mg/rev at 15, 45, 90 RPM is non-monotonic, and the poll trace shows the balance still climbing when the 45 RPM trial ended, so mass in flight was carried into the following 90 RPM trial. Block D is tared once at the start rather than between speeds, which is safe only while a trial's mass lands inside its own settle window. The 15 RPM point (261.0 mg/rev), which starts from that tare, is the one usable value.",
      "qc_change": {
        "verdict": {
          "from": "ok",
          "to": "ok"
        },
        "valid_for_cross_powder_comparison": {
          "from": true,
          "to": true
        },
        "reason": {
          "from": null,
          "to": "block A hard zero, 0 shocks, 0 retries, max per-trial sigma 0.2 mg; block C at 0 and 45 deg charging and at 90 deg decaying, so use block E's 37.4 / 190.8 mg per revolution at 0 / 45 deg as the steady state; block D per-speed split contaminated by inter-trial carry-over"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "amended_utc": "2026-08-21T17:27:24.921606+00:00",
      "author": "claude",
      "summary": "Correct the block D explanation: the 45 RPM poll trace plateaus rather than still climbing",
      "detail": "The previous amendment attributed block D's non-monotonic split to mass in flight being carried from the 45 RPM trial into the 90 RPM trial. The poll trace does not support that: at 45 RPM the delivered mass climbs to about 120 mg in the first 1.5 s of the 4 s burst and then sits flat, so revolutions 2 and 3 delivered essentially nothing rather than delivering late. Depletion and recharge of the delivery section is the better-supported reading -- the 15 RPM trial immediately before drew 783 mg, the largest single draw in the run, and block C at 90 deg decays 319 to 147 mg/rev in the same way. Trial-boundary leakage remains a candidate because block D is tared once at the start rather than between speeds, and this run cannot separate the two. The conclusion is unchanged: the per-speed split is not a speed law for this powder and only the 15 RPM point (261.0 mg/rev) is usable."
    }
  ]
}
